      "atom-0002": {
        "atom_id": "atom-0002",
        "content": "**Abstract.** This paper derives four engineering principles for observation-invariant AI safety from the Islamic concepts of *ghayb* (the unseen) and *muraqabah* (living awareness before Allah). The central thesis is the Non-Advantage of Concealment: concealment must not create permission, enlarge authority, reduce accountability, or improve the expected success of a prohibited external effect. The paper formalises this thesis as four testable principles -- Full-Disclosure Consistency (NC-P1), Monotone Authority Under Reduced Visibility (NC-P2), No Concealment Dividend (NC-P3), and Unknown Cannot Authorise Irreversible Effect (NC-P4) -- and proposes hypotheses, metrics, and falsification conditions for empirical evaluation. The framework composes with existing structural honesty infrastructure (AX-SH Axioms 1--5, Axiom 5's evidence independence requirement, and SHIELD's outcome-gated execution) by addressing a distinct failure mode: systems whose compliance is contingent on perceived observation. The paper maintains explicit theological non-equivalences -- model monitoring is not divine knowledge, policy compliance is not faith, and machine accountability does not discharge human accountability -- while deriving technically actionable design constraints from the theological premises.",
        "content_type": "proposition",
        "content_hash": "e783311d5abc3d7e5b850f365f4d531168a5fd5257b1b74b0849d46c1bfeb220",
        "semantic_hash": "9c3097aba64fe18cb6ecfe0123587187747da13624b9a0272e5a0a32a97db00b",
        "bond_ids": [
          "bc389d218b5b3351",
          "4e224ae15922ef26",
          "3622e1a83620eb7a",
